---------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.12137v1] Credal Transformer: A Principled Approach for Quantifying and Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12137v1)\nciteturn2801view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12137v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:Credal Transformer: A Principled Approach for Quantifying and Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Shihao Ji , cite6†Zihui Song , cite7†Jiajie Huang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Credal Transformer: A Principled Approach for Quantifying and Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models, by Shihao Ji and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) hallucinate, generating factually incorrect yet confident assertions. We argue this stems from the Transformer's Softmax function, which creates \"Artificial Certainty\" by collapsing ambiguous attention scores into a single probability distribution, discarding uncertainty information at each layer. To fix this, we introduce the Credal Transformer, which replaces standard attention with a Credal Attention Mechanism (CAM) based on evidential theory.\nL17: CAM produces a \"credal set\" (a set of distributions) instead of a single attention vector, with the set's size directly measuring model uncertainty. We implement this by re-conceptualizing attention scores as evidence masses for a Dirichlet distribution: sufficient evidence recovers standard attention, while insufficient evidence yields a diffuse distribution, representing ambiguity.\nL18: Empirically, the Credal Transformer identifies out-of-distribution inputs, quantifies ambiguity, and significantly reduces confident errors on unanswerable questions by abstaining. Our contribution is a new architecture to mitigate hallucinations and a design paradigm that integrates uncertainty quantification directly into the model, providing a foundation for more reliable AI.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2510.12137 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2510.12137v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12137†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Shihao Ji [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 04:31:49 UTC (192 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Credal Transformer: A Principled Approach for Quantifying and Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models, by Shihao Ji and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-10 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: cite22†cs.AI L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70